# The two kinds that bear demand nobody served. One row per holder filed as `customer` or
# `unrealised`.
#
# Used three ways, so it is a file
#   As a semi-join it says "this layer admits somebody went unserved", which under a clearance fit
#   is a contradiction, since a supply above demand everywhere leaves nothing unmet. As an
#   anti-join it says "this layer admits nobody", which under a measured-zero capacity and a live
#   exposure is a filing with a hole in it. Aggregated it is the quantity that bounds the exposure.
#   One relation, a semi-join, an anti-join and a sum.
#
# The other three kinds are not its complement
#   `booked`, `counterparty` and `people` all name somebody who absorbed the remainder; these two
#   name demand that was never met at all. That is a difference in kind, not in party.
-- pm:HolderKind values `customer` and `unrealised`.
SELECT h.*
FROM (
    :compose(entries/holders.sqlc)
) h
WHERE h.kind IN ('customer', 'unrealised')
